 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_Xmmw5xQYXQbJN3xr",
   "name": "Polynomial Division",
   "definition": null,
   "video_id": "817942571"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Polynomial division"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is polynomial division?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Polynomial division is the process of **dividing two polynomials**\n\n  - This is usually only useful when the **degree of the denominator** is **less than or equal** to the **degree of the numerator**"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Polynomial division** is a method for splitting polynomials into factor pairs\n\n  - (with or without a remainder term)"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2026/07/8013-polynomial-division-negative-terms-1.png",
   "file": "assets/e944ccb64e76-8013-polynomial-division-negative-terms-1.png",
   "alt": "expressing a polynomial as a pair of factors",
   "caption": ""
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The main uses of polynomial division are\n\n  - factorising polynomials\n  - simplifying 'top-heavy' algebraic fractions"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I divide polynomials?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The method used for polynomial division is just like the **long division** method for numbers\n\n  - sometimes called the 'bus stop method'"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/05/2.5.2-Bus-Stop-Div.png",
   "file": "assets/9d0bd1f16fe1-2-5-2-bus-stop-div.png",
   "alt": "numerical division example with bus stop method",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The answer to a polynomial division question is built up term by term\n\n  - Start by dividing by the highest power term\n  - Write out this multiplied by the divisor and subtract"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/05/2.5.2-Polynomial-Division-Illustration_1.png",
   "file": "assets/184ee257f78d-2-5-2-polynomial-division-illustration-1.png",
   "alt": "polynomial division example with bus stop method pt1",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Continue to divide by each reducing power term and subtracting your answer each time"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/05/2.5.2-Polynomial-Division-Illustration_2.png",
   "file": "assets/6d7bfd4a05ba-2-5-2-polynomial-division-illustration-2.png",
   "alt": "polynomial division example with bus stop method pt2",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Continue until you are left with zero"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/05/2.5.2-Polynomial-Division-Illustration_3.png",
   "file": "assets/26f12e7cb5fd-2-5-2-polynomial-division-illustration-3.png",
   "alt": "polynomial division example with bus stop method pt3",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- If the divisor is **not** a **factor** of the **polynomial** then there will be a **remainder** term left at the end of the division"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "For the polynomial `straight f open parentheses x close parentheses space equals space x to the power of 4 plus 11 x squared minus 1`divide `straight f open parentheses x close parentheses` by $x+3$ and write the remainder.\n\n> *Set up the polynomial division ('bus stop')*\n\n> *There is no *$x^{3}$* term so write this as *$0x^{3}$* in the method. There is no *$x$* term so write this as *$0x$* in the method.*\n\n> *The first division step to consider is *$x^{4}\\divx$*.*\n\n![cie-add-maths-polynomial-division-we-solution-a-part-i](assets/d1b3fa7fc523-cie-add-maths-polynomial-division-we-solution-a-.png)\n\n> *.Multiply *$x^{3}$* by *$x+3$* and subtract from *$x^{4}+0x$*.*\n\n![cie-add-maths-polynomial-division-we-solution-a-part-ii](assets/e3bc5a17d29e-cie-add-maths-polynomial-division-we-solution-a-.png)\n\n> *Bring the *$11x^{2}$* down and divide *$-3x^{3}$* by *$x$*. Continue with each step until you are finished.*\n\n![cie-add-maths-polynomial-division-we-solution-a-part-iii](assets/0f87871c0672-cie-add-maths-polynomial-division-we-solution-a-.png)\n\n**Final answer:** **The remainder is 179.**"
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_rvWQVXDsYyFX7d29",
   "name": "Quadratic Divisor",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Quadratic divisor"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is meant by a quadratic divisor?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Polynomial** division usually involves **dividing** by a **linear** term\n\n  - a term of the form `open parentheses x plus p close parentheses` where $p$ is a constant and usually an **integer**\n- It is possible to divide a polynomial by a **quadratic term** (and cubic, etc)\n\n  - this would be a term of the form `open parentheses x squared plus q x plus r close parentheses` where $q$ and $r$ are constants\n  - this is what is meant by a **quadratic divisor**"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I divide by a quadratic divisor?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The process is the same as for a linear divisor\n\n  - However, as $x^{2}$ will not divide into $x$ (in the polynomial division sense at least) the **remainder,** if there is one, could be of **linear form,** i.e. `open parentheses r x plus s close parentheses` where $r$ and $s$ are constants\n  \n    - It is possible that $r=0$ and so the remainder is still a constant"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- Give yourself plenty of room to do polynomial division\n\n  - Not only will this help avoid errors, it will make your working clear\n- If you make a mistake and change something, fine, but if your method starts to get too messy it is best to restart"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "Find the remainder when $x^{4}+4x^{3}-x+1$ is divided by $x^{2}-2x$.\n\n> *Set up the polynomial division ('bus stop') - there is no *$x^{2}$* term so write this as *$0x^{2}$* in the method.*\n\n> *The first division step to consider is *$x^{4}\\divx^{2}$*.*\n\n![bus stop method for polynomial division with a quadratic divisor](assets/99de3d682ba3-cie-addma25-2023-we-solution-for-quadratic-divis.png)\n\n**Final answer:** **The remainder is **$23x+1$**.**"
  }
 ]
}